<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanchanaburi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanchanaburi is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> at the meeting of the Khwae Noi and Khwae Yai rivers, capital of the province of the same name. It guarded the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> through the Three Pagodas Pass, used by Burmese armies in the wars of the eighteenth century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Japanese built the Burma Railway through the area, including the bridge over the Khwae Yai, with prisoners of war and Asian labourers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanchanaburi.jpg" alt="Kanchanaburi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjoertvedt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanchanaburi_city_gate.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kanchanaburi is a town municipality in Kanchanaburi Province, about 120 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and south-east of Erawan National Park. It lies where the Khwae Noi and Khwae Yai rivers converge into the Mae Klong River, on the northern bank of the river. Its position at the edge of a mountain range keeps it much cooler than other provinces of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It has a tropical savanna climate, with dry winters, a very hot April and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season that runs from May to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 18th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> established Kanchanaburi as a defensive outpost against possible Burmese attacks, in what is now Lat Ya Sub-district. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> period through the Thonburi and Rattanakosin periods it remained an important outpost against Burmese invasions. During the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> the town was moved about 16 km to the south-east, to its present site at the confluence of the two rivers. A royal decree of 25 March 1935 set out the area of the town's two subdistricts, Ban Nuea and Ban Tai.</p><p>In 1942 Kanchanaburi was under Japanese control. Asian forced labourers and Allied prisoners of war building the Burma Railway constructed a bridge here, an event later fictionalised in films including The Bridge on the River Kwai (1957). Almost half of the prisoners working on the project died from disease, maltreatment or accidents. There were four prisoner-of-war camps in or near the town, among them Tamarkan, a work camp used to build the bridge. When the railway was finished, most prisoners were concentrated in the Kanchanaburi area, and many were sent on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> or French Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town keeps a memorial and two museums to commemorate the dead of the railway: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>–Burma Railway Centre, opened in March 2003, and the JEATH War Museum, which are devoted to the bridge and the Death Railway. The Kanchanaburi War Cemetery lies in the town, and the Chungkai War Cemetery is about 5 km away. Kanchanaburi is also the birthplace of the Buddhist monk Phrabhavanaviriyakhun and lies close to Wat Tham Phu Wa, a temple with grotto shrines in a limestone cave system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanchanaburi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…broad front in what Thai histories call the Nine Armies War; the Siamese defeated the main thrust at Lat Ya near <b>Kanchanaburi</b>, the attack on Thalang on Phuket island was repulsed, and later Burmese campaigns, the last major one against Thalang in 1809–1810, failed to reverse the result.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
